---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>

  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      For a temporary note, open the notepad and start typing. There is no account and no sign-up.
      The text auto-saves in your browser on this device, so it is still there after you close the tab
      or restart the computer.
    </p>
    <p>
      It does not survive clearing your browser's site data, and it does not sync to other devices.
      Export anything you cannot afford to lose.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Notepad →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="scratchpad-vs-notes-app">
    <h2>When Does a Browser Scratchpad Beat a Notes App? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      When the note is short-lived. A scratchpad is the right place for a phone number you need for
      the next ten minutes, a draft reply you want to rework before sending, text you are cleaning up
      between two apps, or a list for today only.
    </p>
    <p>
      A notes app wins when you need search across hundreds of notes, sync between your phone and
      laptop, or sharing. The scratchpad trades those features for speed: one page, one note, nothing
      to sign in to.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what-survives">
    <h2>What Survives a Restart or Cleared Data? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>The note lives in your browser's local storage, which sets the rules:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Closing the tab or the browser:</strong> the note survives.</li>
      <li><strong>Restarting the computer:</strong> the note survives.</li>
      <li><strong>Clearing cookies and site data:</strong> the note is deleted.</li>
      <li><strong>A private or incognito window:</strong> the note is deleted when the window closes.</li>
      <li><strong>Another browser or device:</strong> the note is not there. Storage belongs to one browser on one device.</li>
      <li><strong>Very long notes:</strong> auto-save stops at 50,000 characters. Beyond that, the saved copy stays at the last shorter version, so download or export long text.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="back-up-notes">
    <h2>How Do You Back Up Notes You Cannot Afford to Lose? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Use the buttons on the tool screen. <strong>Export JSON</strong> saves a backup file that
      <strong>Import JSON</strong> can restore later, either by pasting it in or by choosing the file.
      <strong>Download TXT</strong> saves the plain text, and Ctrl+S (Cmd+S on a Mac) does the same.
    </p>
    <p>
      Make a backup before clearing browser data, switching computers, or leaving a note for more
      than a few days. Once a note matters, move it somewhere permanent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why-notes-matter">
    <h2>Why Notes Matter <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Human working memory (the mental space used for active thinking) is limited.
      Most people can hold only a handful of items at once before earlier ones slip away.
      When you are trying to work and remember things at the same time, both suffer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Writing reduces this pressure. Once an idea or task is captured on paper or in a text field,
      your mind can release it. You stop expending effort on retention and direct that energy toward
      the work itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is sometimes called cognitive offloading: using an external system to carry information
      so your brain does not have to. Notebooks, sticky notes, and browser notepads all serve the
      same purpose: they extend your memory beyond its natural limits.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="The core benefit">
      <p>Notes do not make you smarter. They free your attention so you can think more clearly about one thing at a time, instead of juggling multiple things in your head simultaneously.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="benefits">
    <h2>Benefits <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reduced mental load:</strong> once something is written, your brain stops working to hold it.</li>
      <li><strong>Better recall:</strong> writing reinforces memory, and the note is there if you forget.</li>
      <li><strong>Improved focus:</strong> with stray thoughts captured, you can give one task your full attention.</li>
      <li><strong>Clearer thinking:</strong> putting an idea into words shows where it is still vague.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-take-better-notes">
    <h2>How To Take Better Notes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Capture immediately.</strong> Speed matters more than polish at the capture stage.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep it concise.</strong> "Check router config Thursday" is a better note than a paragraph.</li>
      <li><strong>Write for your future self.</strong> Add enough context to understand it next week.</li>
      <li><strong>Review occasionally.</strong> A quick daily or weekly scan turns notes into action.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Spending more time organizing than capturing">
      <p>
        A perfectly structured note system you rarely use is less valuable than a messy one you
        actually write in. Organization should serve retrieval, not become a goal in itself.
        Start simple and add structure only when you clearly need it.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Trying to remember instead of writing it down">
      <p>
        Relying on memory for things that could be written down creates unnecessary mental load.
        If a thought, task, or idea crosses your mind and matters, write it immediately.
        The cost of a note is seconds. The cost of forgetting can be much higher.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Review is where notes pay off">
      <p>Capturing notes is only half the practice. Reading them back is what turns captured information into something useful. Even a quick daily scan of recent notes is enough to make the habit worthwhile.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How It Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      This notepad saves every keystroke automatically to browser storage: a local area built
      into every modern browser that stays on your device. Auto-save means nothing is lost if
      you close the tab or navigate away. When you return, your notes are exactly where you
      left them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Privacy is built in by design. ToyTools has no server. Your notes never leave your device
      and are never transmitted anywhere. The notepad works offline: no internet connection is required once the page loads.
      For example, you can open the notepad on a flight, take notes throughout, and return to
      find everything saved when you reconnect. No account, no subscription, no setup.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="history">
    <h2>A Brief History of Note-Taking <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      People have kept written records for as long as writing has existed. Ancient merchants
      recorded transactions on clay tablets. Scholars annotated texts in the margins.
      Explorers kept journals. The tools changed (from stone to papyrus to paper) but the
      purpose remained the same: do not rely on memory alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pocket notebook became a widespread personal tool in the nineteenth century as paper
      became cheap and literacy spread. Writers, scientists, and engineers carried them as
      standard equipment. Ideas that would otherwise be lost were captured and developed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Digital note-taking emerged with personal computers and took off with smartphones.
      Today, the same function that once required paper and pen can happen instantly in a browser.
    </p>
  </section>

</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }

  .cta-link:hover {
    opacity: 0.9;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
